## 12. SECURITY / TRUST BOUNDARIES

- **`PermissionGateway.check()` is structurally mandatory** — every single path to a tool handler goes through `ToolRegistry.invoke()`, which calls `check()` unconditionally before the handler runs. No code path bypasses this (confirmed in the prior WATCHER engagement and re-confirmed here — the WATCHER read-back itself goes through a **second**, fully-gated `invoke()` call, not a raw read).
- **`TrustOrigin`** flows unchanged from `Task` creation through `ExecutionContext.trust_origin` into every `check()`/`AuditLog.record()` call (`originator=` parameter) — this identity thread is intact and unmodified by any of the WATCHER or dependency work.
- **`AuditLog`** is append-only (`agent/core/audit.py`), one JSON line per entry, never rewritten/truncated — genuinely tamper-evident in its current form, though its write path has **no lock** (§5) — a real, if narrow, gap for true concurrent writers.
- **The one already-known concurrency gap with direct security relevance:** `PermissionGateway._pending`/`_grants` (§5) are unlocked, and their own code comments confirm the authors know background-executor concurrency already touches them. A future fleet orchestrator that runs more truly-concurrent execution would inherit this exact risk more severely — two simultaneous approval-requiring calls for the same tool/session could race in ways not yet fully hardened.
- **Secrets isolation** (`SecretsVault`, credential-reference pattern) is lock-protected and unaffected by anything in this investigation.
- **For any future parallel-execution design to preserve these boundaries:** the natural, minimal-risk path is to keep `PermissionGateway`/`AuditLog`/stores exactly as the single, shared, already-battle-tested chokepoint they are today (every parallel worker still calls the *same* `ToolRegistry.invoke()` → `PermissionGateway.check()` → `Sandbox` chain) and add whatever locking is missing (§5) rather than duplicating or bypassing any part of this chain for the sake of parallelism. Nothing in the current design gives any indication that duplicating this chain per-worker was ever intended or would be safe.

